(6989916, 1055964, 100393, '<td valign="top"><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1051747" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/100393/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1051747.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/100393/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1051747.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/100393/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1051747.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>Awarded for the February 2013 Free Study. <br/> <br/>This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 10s - I like it very much. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: <br/> <br/>1. Shouts <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=96751" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">tanguera</a> to me. I like images that have a distinct style and this beautiful indoor light, interesting internal space, floating motif, rich colours has all the hallmarks.<br/> <br/>2. Sensational mood - slightly eerie but with undertones of joy too - not an easy thing to pull off.<br/> <br/>3. Amazing coloration - it has a richness that belies its fairly limited colour palette.<br/> <br/>4. Superb light, superbly captured. This image is sooooo exposure critical and you have nailed it.<br/> <br/>5. Composition - leading lines and a great sense of depth - way to use the z axis!<br/> <br/>6. Depth of field - it''s really important that we are able to see the full depth of your image, this would suggest a small aperture.... but you''ve frozen the fabric suggesting a decently quick shutter speed - no noise to speak of, so there was loads of natural light meaning those windows are really, really bright - well done on controlling the exposure across the frame or you''ve illuminated the fabric with a strobe - well done on getting that so well balanced and natural looking.<br/> <br/>Overall, one of those images where everything hangs together beautifully. From photograph to art (without compromising the process, practice and craft) of photography.<br/> <br/>Should ribbon.</td>', '  Awarded for the February 2013 Free Study.  This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 10s - I like it very much. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:  1. Shouts  tanguera to me. I like images that have a distinct style and this beautiful indoor light, interesting internal space, floating motif, rich colours has all the hallmarks. 2. Sensational mood - slightly eerie but with undertones of joy too - not an easy thing to pull off. 3. Amazing coloration - it has a richness that belies its fairly limited colour palette. 4. Superb light, superbly captured. This image is sooooo exposure critical and you have nailed it. 5. Composition - leading lines and a great sense of depth - way to use the z axis! 6. Depth of field - it''s really important that we are able to see the full depth of your image, this would suggest a small aperture.... but you''ve frozen the fabric suggesting a decently quick shutter speed - no noise to speak of, so there was loads of natural light meaning those windows are really, really bright - well done on controlling the exposure across the frame or you''ve illuminated the fabric with a strobe - well done on getting that so well balanced and natural looking. Overall, one of those images where everything hangs together beautifully. From photograph to art (without compromising the process, practice and craft) of photography. Should ribbon.', '2013-03-03 05:48:49.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989918, 1053551, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 10s - I like it very much. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:<br/> <br/>1. I should start by saying that I''m not a ''horsey'' person and for the most part (because of that) my default position isn''t one of enjoying an image of a horse. But... I love this! These horses, a bit like Icelandic horses, are very appealing with their shagginess - this is critical for your photograph because it adds interesting texture and it does great things to the most forward horse''s silhouette against the light - natural feathering.<br/> <br/>2. The overall composition with the aligned animals leads the eye into the picture and gives it depth.<br/> <br/>3. The toning is beautiful - I wonder if there is the slightest haloing under the chest of the foreground horse but it is countered by a sense of mist.<br/> <br/>4. you captured that combination of glow and sharpness that I enjoy in <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_N.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=113156" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">jagar</a>''s work (though of course with softer tones).<br/> <br/>5. Then there is the interloping goat! This fellow makes the image, like a nerd at a party trying to hang out with the cool kids in the kitchen (I know the feeling well), he is as endearing as he is funny and he he is oh-so-important to your picture.<br/> <br/>6. The shot has a sense of humour but, through you great processing, is nonetheless highly credible as a serious photograph too.<br/> <br/>Overall, a fantastically well-balance shot.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 10s - I like it very much. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: 1. I should start by saying that I''m not a ''horsey'' person and for the most part (because of that) my default position isn''t one of enjoying an image of a horse. But... I love this! These horses, a bit like Icelandic horses, are very appealing with their shagginess - this is critical for your photograph because it adds interesting texture and it does great things to the most forward horse''s silhouette against the light - natural feathering. 2. The overall composition with the aligned animals leads the eye into the picture and gives it depth. 3. The toning is beautiful - I wonder if there is the slightest haloing under the chest of the foreground horse but it is countered by a sense of mist. 4. you captured that combination of glow and sharpness that I enjoy in  jagar''s work (though of course with softer tones). 5. Then there is the interloping goat! This fellow makes the image, like a nerd at a party trying to hang out with the cool kids in the kitchen (I know the feeling well), he is as endearing as he is funny and he he is oh-so-important to your picture. 6. The shot has a sense of humour but, through you great processing, is nonetheless highly credible as a serious photograph too. Overall, a fantastically well-balance shot.', '2013-03-03 06:15:43.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989920, 1055769, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:<br/> <br/>1. I like purposeful choices in photographs - it''s clear you''ve chosen this high contrast style and it suits the image wonderfully week - makes it really.<br/> <br/>2. I like to see people in images (mainly) - there are plenty of characters to look at here; and in your composition, all individuals but nonetheless together in common purpose.<br/> <br/>3. I like how you''ve cropped it, not minding if your crop people - it creates a sense of a wider group and mimics the way our eye is naturally selective in its gaze - we are aware of others but aren''t looking at them.<br/> <br/>Overall, this is a great image - made so by your photographic choices. We see what you have brought to the photographic table. Excellent.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: 1. I like purposeful choices in photographs - it''s clear you''ve chosen this high contrast style and it suits the image wonderfully week - makes it really. 2. I like to see people in images (mainly) - there are plenty of characters to look at here; and in your composition, all individuals but nonetheless together in common purpose. 3. I like how you''ve cropped it, not minding if your crop people - it creates a sense of a wider group and mimics the way our eye is naturally selective in its gaze - we are aware of others but aren''t looking at them. Overall, this is a great image - made so by your photographic choices. We see what you have brought to the photographic table. Excellent.', '2013-03-03 06:26:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989921, 1055403, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:<br/> <br/>1. Bird shots aren''t normally the sorts of images that would catch my eye when doing these types of comments, don''t get me wrong - I can appreciate them, they just aren''t what I''m looking for. This one is another story though. The light and the way you''ve used and captured it takes this to another level.<br/> <br/>2. Fabulous texture - would''ve been easy to over do the structure/sharpening; but you haven''t. It''s perfect.<br/> <br/>3. Super, subtle coloration - so glad it''s there though. In monochrome, this would be much-diminished.<br/> <br/>4. The ''nobility'' of the bird, or at least that which you have given imbued it with is compelling.<br/> <br/>Overall - how to do a bird study!</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: 1. Bird shots aren''t normally the sorts of images that would catch my eye when doing these types of comments, don''t get me wrong - I can appreciate them, they just aren''t what I''m looking for. This one is another story though. The light and the way you''ve used and captured it takes this to another level. 2. Fabulous texture - would''ve been easy to over do the structure/sharpening; but you haven''t. It''s perfect. 3. Super, subtle coloration - so glad it''s there though. In monochrome, this would be much-diminished. 4. The ''nobility'' of the bird, or at least that which you have given imbued it with is compelling. Overall - how to do a bird study!', '2013-03-03 06:34:20.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989923, 1055873, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: <br/> <br/>1. I love how you''ve seen the potential in this scene and endeavoured to make a great photograph of it. The low light and consequent noise has no doubt given you a problem but you''ve brought you vision and editing skills to bear and I do believe we have an optimised image. I really like it when it''s clear that photographer choices have a profound influence on what we are seeing.<br/> <br/>2. This high contrast monochrome look is just perfect - it creates a great mood.<br/> <br/>3. The canopy is a magnificent device in your image - it adds depth and perspective to a scene that might have otherwise been flat. The way you have framed to it and brought out its contrast is highly effective.<br/> <br/>4. I like how you''ve transformed the scene - an ordinary food court now the source of vibrant night-time entertainment!<br/> <br/>Overall - really well done, I like how ''crafted'' this is.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:  1. I love how you''ve seen the potential in this scene and endeavoured to make a great photograph of it. The low light and consequent noise has no doubt given you a problem but you''ve brought you vision and editing skills to bear and I do believe we have an optimised image. I really like it when it''s clear that photographer choices have a profound influence on what we are seeing. 2. This high contrast monochrome look is just perfect - it creates a great mood. 3. The canopy is a magnificent device in your image - it adds depth and perspective to a scene that might have otherwise been flat. The way you have framed to it and brought out its contrast is highly effective. 4. I like how you''ve transformed the scene - an ordinary food court now the source of vibrant night-time entertainment! Overall - really well done, I like how ''crafted'' this is.', '2013-03-03 06:47:53.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989924, 1055362, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:<br/> <br/>1. Has an <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=75618" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">odriew</a> feel about it - the coloration, the artsy person in the environment motif. I like it very much.<br/> <br/>2. It''s contrived - I mean that in a good way - I like how this is constructed, not accidental - the scene only exists because of you, you aren''t a (near) passive reporter of the world.<br/> <br/>3. It took me a while to realise they were jumping, I enjoyed the ''aha'' moment.<br/> <br/>4. I like how the stratified sweater mirror the stratification of colours in the sky behind.<br/> <br/>5. The held foliage certainly works and does add interest. I did wonder whether it takes it over the edge a little - but in the end I didn''t care.<br/> <br/>Overall: A well crafted image that I have enjoyed and certainly the sort of image I''d like to see more often here at DPC.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: 1. Has an  odriew feel about it - the coloration, the artsy person in the environment motif. I like it very much. 2. It''s contrived - I mean that in a good way - I like how this is constructed, not accidental - the scene only exists because of you, you aren''t a (near) passive reporter of the world. 3. It took me a while to realise they were jumping, I enjoyed the ''aha'' moment. 4. I like how the stratified sweater mirror the stratification of colours in the sky behind. 5. The held foliage certainly works and does add interest. I did wonder whether it takes it over the edge a little - but in the end I didn''t care. Overall: A well crafted image that I have enjoyed and certainly the sort of image I''d like to see more often here at DPC.', '2013-03-03 06:57:26.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989929, 1052720, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:<br/> <br/>1. This is a great arty image - excellent muted colours and an interesting glowing blur treatment.<br/> <br/>2. The overall composition is a standout - the way the frame is broken up into sections is very interesting.<br/> <br/>3. It does have a constructed look but it looks candid too - the best of both worlds.<br/> <br/>4. The more I look, the more I want to look - that''s got to be a good thing.<br/> <br/>Overall, a very well crafted image with tons of interest.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: 1. This is a great arty image - excellent muted colours and an interesting glowing blur treatment. 2. The overall composition is a standout - the way the frame is broken up into sections is very interesting. 3. It does have a constructed look but it looks candid too - the best of both worlds. 4. The more I look, the more I want to look - that''s got to be a good thing. Overall, a very well crafted image with tons of interest.', '2013-03-03 07:17:14.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989938, 1054986, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:<br/> <br/>1. I like quirky portraits and this one certainly qualifies - you''ve made some really good choices here. This feels like a <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=102278" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">rooum</a> but I''m not sure why.<br/> <br/>2. The DOF intrigues me - I think it is lens induced - looks a bit like what I get with my 85mm f/1.2 but the fall off seems to be even greater.<br/> <br/>3. DOF aside, where you''ve placed the plane of focus is just perfect - passing straight through the irises.<br/> <br/>3. The monochrome toning is perfect - just a hit of sepia.<br/> <br/>4. The deadpan expression of your subject is highly compelling - engaged but somehow dismissive.<br/> <br/>Overall, a fine piece of work.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: 1. I like quirky portraits and this one certainly qualifies - you''ve made some really good choices here. This feels like a  rooum but I''m not sure why. 2. The DOF intrigues me - I think it is lens induced - looks a bit like what I get with my 85mm f/1.2 but the fall off seems to be even greater. 3. DOF aside, where you''ve placed the plane of focus is just perfect - passing straight through the irises. 3. The monochrome toning is perfect - just a hit of sepia. 4. The deadpan expression of your subject is highly compelling - engaged but somehow dismissive. Overall, a fine piece of work.', '2013-03-03 07:24:55.000000', NULL, 1),
(6989942, 1055817, 100393, '<td valign="top">This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style! <br/> <br/>(Voted earlier) <br/> <br/>This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me: <br/> <br/>1. I really enjoy the relationship story here - i makes me want to watch them for a while.<br/> <br/>2. The composition is witty and effective - particularly in how the carpeted steps merges into her top. I also like how we, as viewers, get pulled into the scene - you''ve included enough physical context for our brains to be easily located within in the scene - we know exactly where we are in the room.<br/> <br/>3. Excellent monochrome treatment.<br/> <br/>Overall, a compelling candid image.</td>', 'This is a new comment series for me, so please bear with me while I find my style!  (Voted earlier)  This is one of my 9s - I like it a lot. Furthermore I think it enriches the DPC collective portfolio. Put simply, I wish we saw more stuff like this. Let me tell you why it pushes all the right buttons for me:  1. I really enjoy the relationship story here - i makes me want to watch them for a while. 2. The composition is witty and effective - particularly in how the carpeted steps merges into her top. I also like how we, as viewers, get pulled into the scene - you''ve included enough physical context for our brains to be easily located within in the scene - we know exactly where we are in the room. 3. Excellent monochrome treatment. Overall, a compelling candid image.', '2013-03-03 07:28:37.000000', NULL, 1),
